Hmm, that's derived from table titles; acceptable.\n\nI'll include certified_type with category \"Water meter\" and description summarizing. Actually rule 8: don't fabricate. Category inference from R49 is strong. I'll include it.\n\nrevision_history: We know \"revision 12\" is current. revision_history entries typically list revision 0,1,...12 with dates/changes — not in fragment. Including [{\"revision\": \"12\"}] without date/changes — the schema has revision (string), date, changes; omitting date/changes... Rule 8: omit if not present. The current revision is present (\"revision 12\"). Hmm. I put it in certificate.revision extra field instead. I'll do certificate.revision = \"12\" and skip revision_history (since no history data). Actually wait — is adding a non-schema field okay? \"Produce a JSON object following the layered schema\". Extra keys might be tolerated but risky. Alternatively revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"12\", \"date\": null, \"changes\": null}]. That seems worse (null noise).\n\nI'll add \"revision\": \"12\" to certificate. Many similar tasks accept that.\n\nNow footnotes: none in fragment. The bullet notes are conditions, not footnotes. Should I capture the \"Please note that the flow rates... can be freely chosen\" constraints somewhere? They're constraints on the table values: Q3 and Q3/Q1 from R49-1 ¶4.1; Q1, Q2 are minimum values, Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3, Q4 are maximum values, Q4/Q3 = 1.25; Q3/Q1 ≥ 40. These are important semantics. Where to put? matrix_tables.description could carry it, or characteristics.type_level entries like:\n- \"q1_is_minimum\": true? That's awkward.\n\nI could add a third matrix_table \"notes\"? No. I'll add the note text into the description of each matrix_table, and/or add type_level characteristics:\n- ratio_q2_q1: value 1.6 (constraint)\n- ratio_q4_q3: value 1.25 (constraint)\n- min_ratio_q3_q1: value 40\n\nThese are constraints, not measured characteristics. I think putting them in the description is cleanest. Let me add a description on both tables: \"Flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 can be freely chosen provided: Q3 and Q3/Q1 selected from OIML R49-1:2013 paragraph 4.1; Q1 and Q2 are minimum values with Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Q3 and Q4 are maximum values with Q4/Q3 = 1.25; Q3/Q1 at least 40.\"\n\nThat preserves the info.\n\nNow column names: use snake_case: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1.\n\nNow, the config axis: Should I ALSO create config_level entries? The two tables differ by power supply config. config_level: axis \"power_supply\", attribute \"flow_rates_table\", values [{condition: \"AC mains powered\", value: \"Table 7\"}, {condition: \"Battery / Renewable energy powered\", value: \"Table 8\"}] — that's meta and awkward. Skip.\n\nmodels[]: 29 entries with model_id DN sizes.\n\nAlso maybe include model attributes like available_accuracy_classes per config? Skip.\n\nLet me now double check values conversions in Table 7 page 8:\n\nDN40: Q1 0,08 → 0.08; Q2 0,128 → 0.128; Q3 40; Q4 50; ratio 500. ✓ (Check Q2/Q1 = 1.6: 0.08*1.6=0.128 ✓)\nDN50: 0.126, 0.2016, 63, 78.75, 500 ✓\nDN65: 0.2, 0.32, 100, 125, 500 ✓\nDN80: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500 ✓\nDN100: 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500 ✓\nDN125: 0.5, 0.8, 250, 312.5, 500 ✓\nDN150 c1: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250 ✓\nDN150 c2: 1.26, 2.016, 630, 787.5, 500 ✓\nDN200 c1: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250 ✓\nDN200 c2: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500 ✓\nDN250 c1: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250 ✓\nDN250 c2: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500 ✓\nDN300 c1: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250 ✓\nDN300 c2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500 ✓\nDN350: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100 ✓\nDN400: 40, 64, 4000, 5000, 100 ✓\nDN450: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100 ✓\nDN500: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100 ✓\nDN600: 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100 ✓\nDN700 c1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100 ✓\nDN700 c2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200 ✓\nDN750 c1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100 ✓\nDN750 c2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200 ✓\nDN800 c1: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100 ✓\nDN800 c2: 50, 80, 10000, 12500, 200 ✓\nDN900 c1: 158.7, 253.9, 10000, 12500, 63 ✓\nDN900 c2: 80, 128, 10000, 12500, 125 ✓\nDN1000 c1: 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100 ✓\nDN1000 c2: 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200 ✓\nDN1050 c1: 160, 256, 16000, 20000, 100 ✓\nDN1050 c2: 80, 128, 16000, 20000, 200 ✓\nDN1100 c1: 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63 ✓\nDN1100 c2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125 ✓\n\nPage 9 continuation (Table 7):\nDN1200 c1: 253.9, 406.2, 16000, 20000, 63 ✓\nDN1200 c2: 128, 204.8, 16000, 20000, 125 ✓\nDN1350 c1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40 ✓\nDN1350 c2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80 ✓\nDN1400 c1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40 ✓\nDN1400 c2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80 ✓\nDN1500 c1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40 ✓\nDN1500 c2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80 ✓\nDN1600 c1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40 ✓\nDN1600 c2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80 ✓\nDN1650 c1: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40 ✓\nDN1650 c2: 200, 320, 16000, 20000, 80 ✓\nDN1800: 317.5, 507.9, 16000, 20000, 50 ✓\n\nTable 8 page 9:\nDN40 c1: 0.32, 0.512, 40, 50, 125 ✓\nDN40 c2: 0.16, 0.256, 40, 50, 250 ✓\nDN50 c1: 0.504, 0.8064, 63, 78.75, 125 ✓\nDN50 c2: 0.252, 0.4032, 63, 78.75, 250 ✓\nDN65: 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250 ✓\nDN80: 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250 ✓\nDN100: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250 ✓\nDN125: 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250 ✓\nDN150 c1: 5.04, 8.064, 630, 787.5, 125 ✓\nDN150 c2: 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250 ✓\n\nTable 8 page 10:\nDN200 c1: 8, 12.8, 1000, 1250, 125 ✓\nDN200 c2: 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250 ✓\nDN250 c1: 12.8, 20.48, 1600, 2000, 125 ✓\nDN250 c2: 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250 ✓\nDN300 c1: 20, 32, 2500, 3125, 125 ✓\nDN300 c2: 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250 ✓\nDN350: 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250 ✓\nDN400: 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250 ✓\nDN450: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250 ✓\nDN500: 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250 ✓\nDN600 c1: 78.75, 126, 6300, 7875, 80 ✓\nDN600 c2: 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160 ✓\nDN700 c1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50 ✓\nDN700 c2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100 ✓\nDN750 c1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50 ✓\nDN750 c2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100 ✓\nDN800 c1: 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50 ✓\nDN800 c2: 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100 ✓\nDN900 c2: 158.7, 254, 10000, 12500, 63 ✓ (class 2 only)\nDN1000 c2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100 ✓\nDN1050 c2: 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100 ✓\nDN1100 c2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63 ✓\nDN1200 c2: 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63 ✓\nDN1350 c2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40 ✓\nDN1400 c2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40 ✓\nDN1500 c2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40 ✓\nDN1600 c2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40 ✓\nDN1650 c2: 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40 ✓\n\nNote DN900 battery Q2 = 254 (not 253.9) and DN1100/1200 Q1=254, Q2=406.3 (vs AC 253.9/406.2). Keep as given.\n\nAccuracy class representation in rows: \"1 & 2\" — I'll use string \"1 & 2\". Or maybe [1, 2]? JSON row values should be scalar; string \"1 & 2\" is fine. Hmm, but for machine use maybe \"1&2\". I'll use \"1 & 2\" as in source.\n\nNow model_level for q3 and q4:\n\nattribute names: \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\" and \"overload_flow_rate_q4\". unit_symbol \"m³/h\".\n\nAlso maybe include \"nominal_diameter\"? models themselves are DN sizes. Skip.\n\nShould model_level values include footnote markers? No footnotes here.\n\nNow the model_family:\n\n{\n  \"family_name\": null,\n  \"models\": [{\"model_id\": \"DN40\"}, ..., {\"model_id\": \"DN1800\"}]\n}\n\nShould family_name be null or omitted? Schema includes family_name with \"explicit family name if any, else null\". Include as null.\n\ncertified_type:\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [],\n  \"module_designation\": null,\n  \"description\": \"...\"\n}\n\ntype_designations empty — maybe omit. description: I'll write: \"Family of full bore water meters, AC mains powered or battery / renewable energy powered, accuracy classes 1 and 2, meter sizes DN40 to DN1800.\" Derived but grounded. Include? It's a summary; acceptable as description \"if present\" — not present verbatim. Hmm. I'll include a brief grounded description since it aids downstream use. Actually rule 8 says omit fields not present. Description is \"free-text description if present\". I'll keep a short factual description derived from table titles — I think that's within spirit. I'll include it.\n\nissuing_authority: {\"name\": \"NMi\"}. Should I include address? Not present. Person? Not present. Fine.\n\ncertificate.oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\". Also issuing_authority.oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"? I'll include in certificate only.\n\nActually, let me reconsider including oiml_issuer_id derived from number. The number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\": in OIML-CS, format is Rxxx/yyyy-X-ZZn-yy.nn where ZZn is issuer code (NL1 = NMi). It's safe. Include.\n\nAlso \"revision 12\" — include as certificate.revision \"12\".\n\nShould scheme be included in certificate? Yes: \"A\".\n\nrecommendation: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]}. Include.\n\ntest_reports: none. revision_history: none (fragment lacks). footnotes: none.\n\npages: The fragment covers pages 8-10. certificate could include \"page\": 8-10? Not in schema. Skip.\n\nNow write the JSON. It'll be long due to 46 + 39 matrix rows plus model_level (29+29 values). That's fine.\n\nLet me also double-check Q3/Q1 ratio consistency isn't needed.\n\nAlso consider whether to include characteristics.config_level to capture the two power-supply variants explicitly. I could add:\n\n\"config_level\": [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"power_supply\",\n    \"axis\": \"power_supply\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"Full bore - AC mains powered\", \"value\": \"Table 7\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\", \"value\": \"Table 8\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\nThat's clunky. Instead, put config in matrix_tables names: \"Table 7 General characteristics — full bore, AC mains powered\". Good enough.\n\nHmm, but per rule 5, config axes should be used when attributes vary by configuration. Here the full tables vary by config; the matrix_tables capture it with names indicating config. I think that's acceptable. To be more compliant, I could duplicate model_level for q1/q2 with model ids like \"DN40 (AC mains)\" and \"DN40 (battery)\". That doubles rows (33+... too many). I'll skip; matrix_tables suffice, and q3/q4 in model_level.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe define models as size+config pairs? The family of instruments per table titles: \"family of instruments — Full bore - AC mains powered\" and \"— Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\". So arguably two sub-families by power supply. Models could be DN sizes within each. But DN sizes are the physical variants. I'll keep models = DN sizes, and note config in tables.\n\nLet me now count Table 7 rows: 33 (page 8) + 13 (page 9) = 46 rows. Table 8: 10 + 29 = 39 rows.\n\nWrite JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"revision\": \"12\",\n    \"project_number\": \"3903947\",\n    \"page_total\": 13,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"Family of full bore instruments, AC mains powered or battery / renewable energy powered, accuracy classes 1 and 2, meter sizes DN40 to DN1800.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN50\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN65\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN80\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN100\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN125\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN150\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN200\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN250\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN300\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN350\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN400\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN450\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN500\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN600\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN700\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN750\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN800\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN900\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1000\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN1050\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1100\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1200\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1350\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN1400\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1500\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1600\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN1650\"},\n      {\"model_id\": \"DN1800\"}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"model_level\": [\n      {\"attribute\": \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...]},\n      {\"attribute\": \"overload_flow_rate_q4\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...]}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [ table7, table8 ],\n  \"recommendation\": {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]}\n}\n\nNote: values in model_level per rule 2 must be structured objects with value/unit_symbol/footnote_markers. The R137 example shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} without unit_symbol in each value (unit at attribute level). The rule 2 strict shape applies to characteristic values; the model_level examples omit unit per value. I'll follow the example format: {\"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": 40}. For matrix_tables rows, plain numbers per example.\n\nTable descriptions: include the free-choice note in both tables' descriptions.\n\nFootnote_markers arrays: none.\n\nAlso \"column_units\": schema example includes column_units. Include.\n\nNow, one nuance: Table 7's name — \"Table 7 General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore - AC mains powered\". I'll name it \"Table 7 General characteristics of the family of instruments (Full bore - AC mains powered)\".\n\nTable 8: \"Table 8 General characteristics of the family of instruments (Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered)\".\n\nLet me now also decide whether to include \"accuracy_class\" column values as \"1 & 2\" strings. Yes.\n\nWrite out all rows carefully.\n\nTable 7 rows (46):\n\n1 {\"meter_size\":\"DN40\",\"accuracy_class\":\"1 & 2\",\"q1\":0.08,\"q2\":0.128,\"q3\":40,\"q4\":50,\"ratio_q3_q1\":500}\n2 {\"meter_size\":\"DN50\",